## HOW CAN WE HANDLE THE INCREASED WORKLOAD ?
-------------------------------------------------

Ab tak humne dekha ki jaise-jaise application par traffic increase hota gaya,
ek single server ke limited resources gradually exhaust hone lage.

Result:

        Existing Workload

                >

        Server Capacity


Question:

Ab company ke paas kya options hain?

Generally do approaches available hoti hain.


## OPTION-1
------------

Existing server ke Hardware Resources ko increase kar do.

Example:

CPU

    2 Cores

        ↓

    16 Cores

RAM

    4 GB

        ↓

    64 GB

Network

    1 Gbps

        ↓

    10 Gbps

Yaani existing machine ko aur powerful bana diya.

Is approach ko

    Vertical Scaling (Scale Up)

kehte hain.



## OPTION-2
------------

Ek hi powerful server ko continuously upgrade karne ke bajay,

application ke multiple instances different servers par deploy kar diye jaate hain.

Example:

Initially

                Users
                  |
                  |
                  v
            ----------------
            | Spring Boot  |
            | Application  |
            ----------------
                  |
                  v
               Database



After Scaling

                   Users
                      |
                      |
                      v
               Load Balancer
              /      |      \
             /       |       \
            v        v        v

      SpringBoot SpringBoot SpringBoot
        Server-1   Server-2   Server-3
             \        |        /
              \       |       /
               --------|-------
                      |
                  Database


Load Balancer incoming requests ko available application
instances ke beech distribute karta hai.

Is approach ko

    Horizontal Scaling (Scale Out)

kehte hain.


## WHICH APPROACH SHOULD WE CHOOSE ?
--------------------------------------

Is question ka koi universal answer nahi hai.

Ye mainly following factors par depend karta hai:

1. CURRENT TRAFFIC
-------------------
Agar traffic abhi kam hai, to Vertical Scaling sufficient ho sakti hai.
Agar traffic bahut zyada hai, to Horizontal Scaling ki zarurat pad sakti hai.


2. FUTURE TRAFFIC GROWTH
--------------------------
Agar expected hai ki users continuously increase honge,
to Horizontal Scaling generally better option hoti hai.


3. DOWNTIME TOLERANCE
-----------------------
Agar application ko server upgrade ke time kuch downtime allow hai,
to Vertical Scaling consider ki ja sakti hai.

Agar application ko continuously available rakhna hai,
to Horizontal Scaling generally preferred hoti hai.


4. BUDGET
-----------
Kabhi-kabhi ek powerful server lena sasta pad sakta hai.

Kabhi multiple normal servers use karna better option hota hai.

Ye infrastructure cost par depend karta hai.


5. APPLICATION ARCHITECTURE
-----------------------------

Kuch applications ko horizontally scale karna relatively easy hota hai.

Kuch applications ko horizontally scale karne ke liye additional changes karne padte hain.


## WHY DOES THIS CREATE A PROBLEM IN HORIZONTAL SCALING?
---------------------------------------------------------

Horizontal Scaling sirf multiple application instances
deploy kar dene se complete nahi ho jaati.

Suppose application ke multiple instances chal rahe hain.

                   Load Balancer
                  /      |      \
                 /       |       \
                v        v        v

            Server-1  Server-2  Server-3

User ne login kiya.

Load Balancer ne login request

    Server-1

par bhej di.

Server-1 ne HttpSession create ki aur
user ka login session apni local memory (RAM)
me store kar diya.

Ab user ki next request aayi.

Is baar Load Balancer ne request

    Server-2

ko forward kar di.

Problem:

Server-2 ke paas Server-1 wali HttpSession
available hi nahi hai,

kyunki wo sirf Server-1 ki local memory
me stored thi.

Result:

Server-2 ko lagega ki user login hi nahi hai.

User ko dobara login karna pad sakta hai,
ya request fail ho sakti hai (application implementation par depend karta hai).

Isi wajah se Horizontal Scaling ke liye
sirf multiple application instances deploy
kar dena enough nahi hota.

Application ko bhi horizontally scalable
banana padta hai.

Common Solutions:

    - Stateless Design (Most Preferred)
    - External Session Store (Redis)
    - Sticky Sessions (Less Preferred)



## WHAT IS HTTP SESSION ?
---------------------------

HTTP / HTTPS (HTTP + TLS/SSL) protocol by default stateless hota hai.

Matlab server ko by default ye yaad nahi rehta ki
current request kis user ne bheji hai aur kya us user ne
isse pehle koi request bheji thi.

Lekin real-world applications me hume user ki kuch information
multiple requests ke beech maintain karni padti hai.


HttpSession ek server-side object (ya simple language me
ek "container") hai jisme application user ke login ke
baad uski session-related information ko multiple HTTP
requests ke beech temporarily store karti hai.


User Login
    ↓
Tomcat creates HttpSession
    ↓
Application stores data inside HttpSession


Application pehle user ko authenticate karti hai
(i.e. verify karti hai ki username/password ya credentials
valid hain ya nahi).

Authentication successful hone ke baad application
generally HttpSession create karti hai
(through the Servlet Container such as Tomcat)
aur user ki session-related information usme store kar deti hai.

Baad me aane wali requests me isi session ki help se
user ko identify (Authentication Context) kiya jata hai,
aur uske Roles / Permissions ke basis par Authorization
perform ki ja sakti hai.


HttpSession user-related koi bhi temporary information
store kar sakta hai, jaise:

├── Logged-in User ID
├── Username
├── User Roles (ADMIN / USER)
├── Authentication Status
├── Shopping Cart
├── User Preferences (Language, Theme)
├── Multi-step Form Data
├── CSRF Token
├── Temporary OTP Verification State
└── Any other temporary user-specific data

Isi purpose ke liye HttpSession use kiya jata hai.


## IMPORTANT:
---------------

HttpSession khud koi storage technology (jaise Redis,
Database, ya File System) nahi hai.

Ye sirf ek Session Object hai.

By default, Tomcat jaise Servlet Containers is Session Object
ko server ki local memory (RAM) me store karte hain.

Is approach ki kuch limitations hain:

1. Agar application multiple servers par deploy ho,
   to default in-memory HttpSession Horizontal Scaling
   ke saath problems create kar sakta hai.

   Kyunki har server ki apni separate local memory hoti hai,
   isliye ek server par create hua HttpSession automatically
   doosre server ko available nahi hota.

2. Agar server restart ya crash ho jaye,
   to us server ki local memory clear ho jaati hai.

   Is wajah se saare in-memory HttpSessions destroy ho jaate hain,
   aur users ko dobara login karna pad sakta hai.

Isi wajah se modern distributed applications
generally Stateless Authentication (JWT) ya
External Session Store (e.g. Redis) prefer karti hain.


## HOW DOES HTTP SESSION WORK ?
--------------------------------

Suppose user login karta hai.

Flow:

        User
          |
          | Login Request
          v
Spring Boot + Tomcat


Application pehle user ko authenticate karti hai
(i.e. verify karti hai ki username/password ya credentials
valid hain ya nahi).

Authentication successful hone ke baad application
generally HttpSession create karti hai
(through the Servlet Container such as Tomcat).

Tomcat us session ko ek unique Session ID assign karta hai.

Ye mapping internally maintain ki jaati hai:

        Session ID
             ↓
     HttpSession Object


Tomcat ke paas internally ek Session Store hota hai.


